    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iran's media landscape connects newspapers, broadcasting, digital publishing, audiovisual production, and communication networks. Its development reflects both cultural innovation and recurring struggles over ownership, access, editorial authority, and the circulation of information across borders.",
    "tags": ["Journalism", "Broadcasting", "Digital media", "Publishing", "Communication", "Content production"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T08:01:42Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T08:01:42Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Media, Communication & Content in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "An introduction to Iran's press, broadcasting, digital media, content production, publishing, and the institutions and networks shaping information."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "reading-the-landscape",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A landscape of institutions, formats, and networks",
        "content": "Understanding Iranian media requires attention to both the organizations producing information and the channels carrying it. Newspapers and broadcasting have distinct institutional histories. Online media introduce additional intermediaries, including platforms and network providers. A story's existence, its potential audience, and its actual accessibility are therefore separate questions. Historical scholarship documents the development of print and broadcasting, while internet-freedom research shows how access controls shape digital circulation. [s1][s2][s4]\n\nThis guide approaches media through seven connected branches: news media, radio and television, digital media, content production, advertising and marketing, digital publishing, and communication infrastructure. These are complementary perspectives rather than mutually exclusive industries. A single audiovisual work, for example, can be examined as a production, a catalogue item, and a regulated act of distribution. Research on Iranian subscription streaming demonstrates why those distinctions matter. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "print-and-public-debate",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Print culture developed across cities and borders",
        "content": "Iran's newspaper history extends into the Qajar period, the dynasty that governed before the Pahlavis. Negin Nabavi's account describes journalism gradually developing as newspapers multiplied and their purposes diversified. Late nineteenth-century publications addressed education, science, satire, and reform as well as official affairs. Examples from Tabriz and Bushehr show why this history should not be reduced to the capital alone. [s1]\n\nPersian-language newspapers published abroad were also important. Akhtar in Istanbul and Qanun in London operated beyond the immediate reach of court censorship and circulated arguments about reform. Their positions were not identical: the expatriate press contained different degrees of political radicalism. Publication abroad consequently describes a location and an opportunity for expression, rather than a single editorial outlook. [s1]\n\nFor a reader encountering historical newspapers, place of publication, intended readership, sponsorship, and surviving issues all matter. The press was a vehicle for public discussion, but also an institution whose scope depended on changing relationships with authority. This historical perspective helps explain why a newspaper title alone cannot reveal the conditions under which it was produced. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "broadcasting-and-formal-authority",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Broadcasting developed through a different institutional path",
        "content": "Radio Iran was formally inaugurated in Tehran on 24 April 1940. Its programming combined news with music and other material, helping broadcasting become a medium of shared listening rather than simply a spoken newspaper. Television followed a different initial path: the first Iranian television station was established through private enterprise in 1958. These origins are a reminder that present institutional arrangements should not be projected backward across the whole history of broadcasting. [s2]\n\nThe constitutional framework subsequently assigned a particular position to Islamic Republic of Iran Broadcasting, commonly called IRIB. Article 175 places appointment and dismissal of its head with the Leader and provides for supervision by representatives of the presidency, judiciary, and parliament. Appointment and supervision are distinct relationships; neither should be confused with proof of editorial independence in practice. Article 24 separately recognizes press expression subject to exceptions concerning Islamic principles and public rights. These provisions describe formal authority and conditional protections, rather than an unrestricted guarantee. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "broadcasting-constitutional-relationships",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Constitutional appointment and supervision of broadcasting",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  L[\"Leader\"] -->|\"Appoints and dismisses head\"| B[\"IRIB leadership\"]\n  P[\"President\"] -->|\"Two representatives\"| C[\"Supervisory council\"]\n  J[\"Head of judiciary\"] -->|\"Two representatives\"| C\n  M[\"Parliament\"] -->|\"Two representatives\"| C\n  C -->|\"Supervises functioning\"| B\n```",
        "summary": "Article 175 distinguishes the Leader's appointment power from supervision by a council drawn from the presidency, judiciary, and parliament. The diagram represents the constitutional arrangement, not an assessment of its effectiveness.",
        "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "digital-production-and-distribution",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Digital distribution changes the route to audiences",
        "content": "Digital media must be understood through both content and access. Freedom House's 2024 assessment documents blocking, unequal treatment of domestic and international traffic, and restrictions affecting online expression. The internet is thus more than a neutral delivery pipe: network policy can influence which services are convenient, affordable, or reachable. These findings describe a particular assessment period and should not be read as an uninterrupted service-status report. [s4]\n\nSubscription video provides another example of distribution's importance. Azade Falaki's research examines Filimo, Namava, and Filmnet as curated services operating within a licensing and censorship environment. Choosing when to watch does not remove the decisions determining which version of a work enters a catalogue. Production, platform selection, and regulatory authorization remain separate parts of the viewing experience. [s5]\n\nThis distinction connects the content-production and digital-publishing branches of the guide. The first asks how a work is made; the second asks how it is prepared, selected, and made available. Advertising and marketing add another question: how audiences are reached and attention is financed. These are useful analytical categories, rather than claims that every Iranian outlet follows the same commercial model.",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "constraints-and-cross-border-reporting",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Formal provisions and documented practice",
        "content": "Independent monitoring describes substantial limits on journalism. Reporters Without Borders' 2025 global analysis placed Iran near the bottom of its press-freedom assessment and identified suppression of critical viewpoints. This is an external organization's assessment, distinct from the constitutional provisions describing expression and institutional authority. Both are necessary to distinguish legal design from documented working conditions. [s6]\n\nRestrictions also affect information moving across borders. In May 2026, the Associated Press reported a directive requiring international news organizations operating in Iran to restrict specified downstream uses of their material. The report described restrictions extending to Israeli media and Persian-language television stations based outside Iran. Such measures concern distribution as well as the initial act of reporting. [s7]\n\nA useful reading practice follows from this landscape: identify the producer, the evidence offered, the publication date, and the conditions of distribution. An official account establishes an institution's stated position; independent reporting can test it, but must also be assessed for sourcing and access. Iran's media are best explored through these concrete relationships, with room for cultural production, specialist reporting, and everyday communication alongside the institutions that constrain them.",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s6", "s7"]
      }
    ],
